const JAVA={"bubble": {"file": "BubbleSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class BubbleSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order. A null or very short array is left unchanged.\n    public static void bubbleSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        int n = arr.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            boolean swapped = false;\n            // After pass i, the i + 1 largest values are in their final place at the right end.\n            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {\n                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {\n                    int temp = arr[j];\n                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];\n                    arr[j + 1] = temp;\n                    swapped = true;\n                }\n            }\n            if (!swapped) {\n                break; // no swaps in a full pass: the array is already sorted\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        bubbleSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"compare": [15], "swap": [16, 17, 18], "passEnd": [22]}, "lineEv": {"2": "compare", "3": "swap", "1": "passEnd"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Compares neighbouring values and swaps them when the left one is larger, so the largest remaining value moves to the right end after every pass. The swapped flag lets the method stop as soon as a full pass makes no swaps.", "best": "O(n)", "avg": "O(n²)", "worst": "O(n²)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "Two nested loops compare up to n(n − 1)/2 pairs, which is quadratic. On already sorted input the first pass makes no swap and the swapped flag ends the method after n − 1 comparisons. Only a few local variables are used, so extra space is constant."}, "selection": {"file": "SelectionSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class SelectionSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order using at most n - 1 swaps.\n    public static void selectionSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        int n = arr.length;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            int minIdx = i; // index of the smallest value seen in arr[i..n-1]\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {\n                    minIdx = j;\n                }\n            }\n            if (minIdx != i) {\n                int temp = arr[i];\n                arr[i] = arr[minIdx];\n                arr[minIdx] = temp;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        selectionSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"passEnd": [11], "compare": [14], "swap": [19, 20, 21]}, "lineEv": {"3": "compare", "4": "swap", "0": "passEnd"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Each pass scans the unsorted part for the smallest value (minIdx) and swaps it into position i. It always scans the whole unsorted part, but makes at most n − 1 swaps.", "best": "O(n²)", "avg": "O(n²)", "worst": "O(n²)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "The inner loop always runs to the end of the array, so about n(n − 1)/2 comparisons happen no matter how the data is ordered. Sorting is done in place with a few variables."}, "insertion": {"file": "InsertionSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class InsertionSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order. arr[0..i-1] is always sorted before step i.\n    public static void insertionSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            // Move arr[i] left, one swap at a time, until it reaches its place.\n            for (int j = i; j > 0; j--) {\n                if (arr[j - 1] > arr[j]) {\n                    int temp = arr[j];\n                    arr[j] = arr[j - 1];\n                    arr[j - 1] = temp;\n                } else {\n                    break; // the left neighbour is not larger: stop\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        insertionSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"passEnd": [10], "compare": [13], "swap": [14, 15, 16]}, "lineEv": {"2": "compare", "3": "swap", "0": "passEnd"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Keeps arr[0..i-1] sorted and moves arr[i] left, swapping with larger neighbours, until it reaches its place. This version swaps adjacent values so each swap can be shown in the visualization.", "best": "O(n)", "avg": "O(n²)", "worst": "O(n²)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "On sorted input each value is compared once with its left neighbour and the inner loop stops at once. On reversed input every value moves all the way left, giving about n²/2 swaps. It works in place."}, "merge": {"file": "MergeSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class MergeSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order. Stable; uses one auxiliary array of size n.\n    public static void mergeSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        int[] temp = new int[arr.length];\n        sort(arr, temp, 0, arr.length - 1);\n    }\n\n    private static void sort(int[] arr, int[] temp, int left, int right) {\n        if (left >= right) {\n            return; // zero or one element is already sorted\n        }\n        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;\n        sort(arr, temp, left, mid);\n        sort(arr, temp, mid + 1, right);\n        merge(arr, temp, left, mid, right);\n    }\n\n    // Merges the sorted ranges arr[left..mid] and arr[mid+1..right].\n    private static void merge(int[] arr, int[] temp, int left, int mid, int right) {\n        int i = left;\n        int j = mid + 1;\n        int k = left;\n        while (i <= mid && j <= right) {\n            if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) {\n                temp[k++] = arr[i++];\n            } else {\n                temp[k++] = arr[j++];\n            }\n        }\n        while (i <= mid) {\n            temp[k++] = arr[i++];\n        }\n        while (j <= right) {\n            temp[k++] = arr[j++];\n        }\n        for (int p = left; p <= right; p++) {\n            arr[p] = temp[p];\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        mergeSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"divide": [18], "compare": [30], "writeLeft": [31], "writeRight": [33], "copyLeft": [37], "copyRight": [40], "merged": [42]}, "lineEv": {"0": "divide", "2": "compare", "4": "merged"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Splits the array at the middle, sorts both halves recursively, then merges the two sorted halves into the temp array and copies the result back. Taking from the left half on ties (<=) keeps the sort stable.", "best": "O(n log n)", "avg": "O(n log n)", "worst": "O(n log n)", "space": "O(n)", "why": "The array is halved about log n times and each level merges n values in total. The temp array of size n is needed for merging, and the recursion adds O(log n) stack frames."}, "quick": {"file": "QuickSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class QuickSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order (in place, not stable).\n    public static void quickSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        sort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);\n    }\n\n    private static void sort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {\n        if (low >= high) {\n            return;\n        }\n        int p = partition(arr, low, high);\n        sort(arr, low, p - 1);\n        sort(arr, p + 1, high);\n    }\n\n    // Lomuto partition: values smaller than the pivot end up left of it.\n    private static int partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {\n        int pivot = arr[high];\n        int i = low; // next slot for a value smaller than the pivot\n        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {\n            if (arr[j] < pivot) {\n                swap(arr, i, j);\n                i++;\n            }\n        }\n        swap(arr, i, high);\n        return i;\n    }\n\n    private static void swap(int[] arr, int a, int b) {\n        int temp = arr[a];\n        arr[a] = arr[b];\n        arr[b] = temp;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        quickSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"pivot": [24], "compare": [27], "swapLeft": [28], "placePivot": [32]}, "lineEv": {"1": "pivot", "2": "compare", "3": "swapLeft", "4": "placePivot"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Uses the last value as the pivot and moves every smaller value to its left (Lomuto partition), then places the pivot at its final index and recursively sorts the two sides.", "best": "O(n log n)", "avg": "O(n log n)", "worst": "O(n²)", "space": "O(log n)", "why": "Balanced partitions give log n levels of n comparisons. If the pivot is always the smallest or largest value (for example an already sorted array with a last-element pivot) the partitions are lopsided and the work becomes n². Extra space is the recursion stack, O(log n) on average."}, "heap": {"file": "HeapSort.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class HeapSort {\n\n    // Sorts arr in ascending order using a max heap. In place, not stable.\n    public static void heapSort(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        int n = arr.length;\n        for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            heapify(arr, n, i);\n        }\n        for (int end = n - 1; end > 0; end--) {\n            swap(arr, 0, end);\n            heapify(arr, end, 0);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Sifts arr[root] down so the subtree rooted at root is a max heap (size = heap length).\n    private static void heapify(int[] arr, int size, int root) {\n        int largest = root;\n        int left = 2 * root + 1;\n        int right = 2 * root + 2;\n        if (left < size && arr[left] > arr[largest]) {\n            largest = left;\n        }\n        if (right < size && arr[right] > arr[largest]) {\n            largest = right;\n        }\n        if (largest != root) {\n            swap(arr, root, largest);\n            heapify(arr, size, largest);\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static void swap(int[] arr, int a, int b) {\n        int temp = arr[a];\n        arr[a] = arr[b];\n        arr[b] = temp;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {8, 3, 6, 2, 9, 1, 5};\n        System.out.println(\"Before: \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n        heapSort(data);\n        System.out.println(\"After:  \" + Arrays.toString(data));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"build": [11], "extractSwap": [15], "extractDone": [16], "compareLeft": [25], "compareRight": [28], "heapSwap": [32]}, "lineEv": {"0": "build", "3": "extractDone"}, "evMarks": {}, "about": "Builds a max heap, then repeatedly swaps the root (largest value) to the end of the heap and sifts the new root down with heapify. The sorted values accumulate at the right end.", "best": "O(n log n)", "avg": "O(n log n)", "worst": "O(n log n)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "Building the heap costs O(n); each of the n − 1 extractions does one heapify that walks at most log n levels. Heapify is recursive here, so the call stack uses O(log n); an iterative version needs O(1)."}, "linear": {"file": "LinearSearch.java", "code": "public class LinearSearch {\n\n    // Returns the index of the first element equal to target, or -1 if it is absent.\n    public static int linearSearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n        if (arr == null) {\n            return -1;\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            if (arr[i] == target) {\n                return i;\n            }\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {12, 7, 21, 4, 16, 9};\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 16: \" + linearSearch(data, 16));\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 5:  \" + linearSearch(data, 5));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"check": [9], "found": [10], "notFound": [13]}, "lineEv": {"1": "check", "2": "found", "3": "notFound"}, "evMarks": {"found": ["check", "found"]}, "about": "Checks each element from left to right and returns the index of the first match, or -1 when the loop ends without a match. It works on unsorted data.", "best": "O(1)", "avg": "O(n)", "worst": "O(n)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "The best case finds the target at index 0. On average about half the array is checked, and in the worst case the target is last or missing so all n elements are checked."}, "binary": {"file": "BinarySearch.java", "code": "public class BinarySearch {\n\n    // arr must be sorted in ascending order. Returns an index of target, or -1 if absent.\n    public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {\n        if (arr == null) {\n            return -1;\n        }\n        int low = 0;\n        int high = arr.length - 1;\n        while (low <= high) {\n            int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n            if (arr[mid] == target) {\n                return mid;\n            } else if (arr[mid] < target) {\n                low = mid + 1;\n            } else {\n                high = mid - 1;\n            }\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] data = {4, 7, 9, 12, 16, 21};\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 16: \" + binarySearch(data, 16));\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 5:  \" + binarySearch(data, 5));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"probe": [11, 12, 14], "found": [13], "goRight": [15], "goLeft": [17], "notFound": [20]}, "lineEv": {"3": "found", "4": "goRight", "5": "goLeft", "6": "notFound"}, "evMarks": {"found": ["probe", "found"], "goRight": ["probe", "goRight"], "goLeft": ["probe", "goLeft"]}, "about": "Requires sorted input. Compares the middle value with the target and discards the half that cannot contain it, so the range [low, high] halves on every probe. mid is computed as low + (high - low) / 2 to avoid integer overflow.", "best": "O(1)", "avg": "O(log n)", "worst": "O(log n)", "space": "O(1)", "why": "The best case hits the middle value on the first probe. Each probe halves the range, so at most about log₂(n) probes are needed. The loop is iterative and uses constant extra space."}, "ref_array": {"file": "ArrayOperations.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\n// Basic operations on a Java array. Arrays have a fixed length, so insertion and deletion build a new array.\npublic class ArrayOperations {\n\n    private static void requireArray(int[] arr) {\n        if (arr == null) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Array must not be null\");\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Visits every element in index order and returns them as text, e.g. \"10 20 30\".\n    public static String traverse(int[] arr) {\n        requireArray(arr);\n        StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            if (i > 0) {\n                text.append(' ');\n            }\n            text.append(arr[i]);\n        }\n        return text.toString();\n    }\n\n    // Returns a new array with value inserted at index (0 .. arr.length); later elements shift right.\n    public static int[] insertAt(int[] arr, int index, int value) {\n        requireArray(arr);\n        if (index < 0 || index > arr.length) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", length: \" + arr.length);\n        }\n        int[] result = new int[arr.length + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {\n            result[i] = arr[i];\n        }\n        result[index] = value;\n        for (int i = index; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            result[i + 1] = arr[i];\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    // Returns a new array without the element at index; later elements shift left.\n    public static int[] deleteAt(int[] arr, int index) {\n        requireArray(arr);\n        if (index < 0 || index >= arr.length) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", length: \" + arr.length);\n        }\n        int[] result = new int[arr.length - 1];\n        for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            if (i != index) {\n                result[j++] = arr[i];\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    // Linear search: index of the first element equal to target, or -1.\n    public static int search(int[] arr, int target) {\n        requireArray(arr);\n        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n            if (arr[i] == target) {\n                return i;\n            }\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    // Overwrites arr[index] in place and returns the old value.\n    public static int update(int[] arr, int index, int value) {\n        requireArray(arr);\n        if (index < 0 || index >= arr.length) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", length: \" + arr.length);\n        }\n        int old = arr[index];\n        arr[index] = value;\n        return old;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[] scores = new int[5];\n        int[] primes = {2, 3, 5, 7, 11};\n        int[] filled = new int[4];\n        Arrays.fill(filled, 9);\n        System.out.println(\"Default values: \" + traverse(scores) + \" | filled: \" + traverse(filled));\n        primes = insertAt(primes, 2, 4);\n        System.out.println(\"After insertAt(2, 4): \" + traverse(primes));\n        primes = deleteAt(primes, 0);\n        System.out.println(\"After deleteAt(0):    \" + traverse(primes) + \", index of 7: \" + search(primes, 7));\n        System.out.println(\"Updated index 1, old value \" + update(primes, 1, 40) + \": \" + traverse(primes));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"traverse": [16, 17, 18, 20], "insert": [31, 32, 33, 35, 36, 37], "delete": [48, 49, 50, 51], "search": [60, 61, 62], "update": [74, 75], "declare": [80, 81, 82, 83]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["traverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["insertAt", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["deleteAt", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["update", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the array", "about": "A Java array has a fixed length and stores its elements next to each other, so reading or writing arr[i] is a single step. Because the length cannot change, insertAt and deleteAt build a new array and copy the elements around the changed position. The main method shows the usual ways to declare and initialize an array.", "why": "Index access and update touch one cell, so they are O(1). Traversal and linear search may visit every element. insertAt and deleteAt allocate a new array and copy n elements, so they are O(n) in every case and need O(n) extra space (an in-place shift in a larger array would use O(1) extra space)."}, "ref_deque": {"file": "CircularDeque.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\n// Double-ended queue in a circular array: add and remove at both ends in O(1).\npublic class CircularDeque {\n    private final int[] data;\n    private int front; // index of the first element\n    private int size;\n\n    public CircularDeque(int capacity) {\n        if (capacity <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Capacity must be positive\");\n        }\n        data = new int[capacity];\n    }\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public boolean isFull() { return size == data.length; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void insertFront(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque overflow\");\n        }\n        front = (front - 1 + data.length) % data.length;\n        data[front] = value;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public void insertBack(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque overflow\");\n        }\n        data[(front + size) % data.length] = value;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int removeFront() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque underflow\");\n        }\n        int value = data[front];\n        front = (front + 1) % data.length;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int removeBack() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque underflow\");\n        }\n        int last = (front + size - 1) % data.length;\n        size--;\n        return data[last];\n    }\n\n    public int peekFront() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque is empty\");\n        }\n        return data[front];\n    }\n\n    public int peekBack() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Deque is empty\");\n        }\n        return data[(front + size - 1) % data.length];\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { // front to back\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            result[i] = data[(front + i) % data.length];\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CircularDeque deque = new CircularDeque(4);\n        deque.insertBack(20);\n        deque.insertBack(30);\n        deque.insertFront(10);\n        System.out.println(\"Deque: \" + Arrays.toString(deque.toArray()) + \", front = \" + deque.peekFront() + \", back = \" + deque.peekBack());\n        System.out.println(\"Removed back \" + deque.removeBack() + \" and front \" + deque.removeFront() + \": \" + Arrays.toString(deque.toArray()));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"overflow": [24, 33], "insertFront": [26, 27, 28], "insertBack": [35, 36], "underflow": [41, 51], "removeFront": [44, 45], "removeBack": [53, 54], "peekFront": [62], "peekBack": [69]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insertFront", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertBack", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["removeFront", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["removeBack", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["peekFront / peekBack", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the array", "about": "A double-ended queue in a fixed circular array. front is the index of the first element and the back is (front + size - 1) % capacity. Adding at the front moves front one step back (wrapping with %); every other operation changes one index and the size.", "why": "Each operation changes front or size and touches one array cell, so none of them depends on how many elements are stored. Wrapping with the % operator reuses freed slots, so nothing is ever shifted."}, "ref_pq": {"file": "MinPriorityQueue.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\n// Priority queue backed by a binary min-heap stored in an array: the smallest value is always at index 0.\npublic class MinPriorityQueue {\n    private int[] heap = new int[8];\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void insert(int value) {\n        if (size == heap.length) {\n            heap = Arrays.copyOf(heap, size * 2);\n        }\n        heap[size] = value;\n        size++;\n        siftUp(size - 1);\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (size == 0) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Priority queue is empty\");\n        }\n        return heap[0];\n    }\n\n    // Removes and returns the smallest value.\n    public int extractMin() {\n        if (size == 0) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Priority queue underflow\");\n        }\n        int min = heap[0];\n        size--;\n        heap[0] = heap[size];\n        siftDown(0);\n        return min;\n    }\n\n    // Moves the value at index i up while it is smaller than its parent.\n    private void siftUp(int i) {\n        while (i > 0) {\n            int parent = (i - 1) / 2;\n            if (heap[parent] <= heap[i]) {\n                break;\n            }\n            swap(i, parent);\n            i = parent;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Moves the value at index i down while a child is smaller.\n    private void siftDown(int i) {\n        while (true) {\n            int left = 2 * i + 1;\n            int right = left + 1;\n            int smallest = i;\n            if (left < size && heap[left] < heap[smallest]) {\n                smallest = left;\n            }\n            if (right < size && heap[right] < heap[smallest]) {\n                smallest = right;\n            }\n            if (smallest == i) {\n                break;\n            }\n            swap(i, smallest);\n            i = smallest;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private void swap(int a, int b) {\n        int temp = heap[a];\n        heap[a] = heap[b];\n        heap[b] = temp;\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { return Arrays.copyOf(heap, size); } // heap order, not sorted\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        MinPriorityQueue queue = new MinPriorityQueue();\n        for (int value : new int[]{50, 20, 40, 10, 30}) {\n            queue.insert(value);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Heap array: \" + Arrays.toString(queue.toArray()) + \", min = \" + queue.peek());\n        StringBuilder order = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            order.append(queue.extractMin()).append(' ');\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Extracted in order: \" + order.toString().trim());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"grow": [14], "insert": [16, 17, 18], "peek": [25], "underflow": [31], "extractMin": [33, 34, 35, 36], "siftUp": [42, 43, 44, 47, 48], "siftDown": [54, 55, 56, 57, 58, 59, 61, 62, 64, 67, 68]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insert", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(1) (O(n) when it grows)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["extractMin", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the heap array", "about": "A binary min-heap in an array: the parent of index i is (i - 1) / 2 and its children are 2i + 1 and 2i + 2, and every parent is not larger than its children, so the smallest value is always at index 0. insert adds at the end and sifts up; extractMin moves the last value to the root and sifts down. The array doubles when it is full.", "why": "The heap is a complete binary tree, so its height is about log2 n and a sift moves along one root-to-leaf path: worst case O(log n). An insert often stops after one or two comparisons, so a typical insert is O(1). extractMin always sifts the replacement root down, so it is O(log n) except for a one-element heap. Doubling the array makes growth O(1) amortized."}, "ref_tree": {"file": "BinaryTree.java", "code": "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\n// General binary tree (no ordering rule). New values fill the first free position in level order,\n// so the tree stays complete; deleting replaces the value with the last node's value.\npublic class BinaryTree {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        Node left;\n        Node right;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node root;\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return root == null; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public int height() { return height(root); }\n\n    private int height(Node node) {\n        return node == null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));\n    }\n\n    public void insert(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        size++;\n        if (root == null) {\n            root = node;\n            return;\n        }\n        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        queue.add(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            Node current = queue.remove();\n            if (current.left == null) {\n                current.left = node;\n                return;\n            }\n            queue.add(current.left);\n            if (current.right == null) {\n                current.right = node;\n                return;\n            }\n            queue.add(current.right);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Searches level by level (a general binary tree has no order to exploit).\n    public boolean contains(int value) {\n        if (root == null) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        queue.add(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            Node current = queue.remove();\n            if (current.value == value) {\n                return true;\n            }\n            if (current.left != null) { queue.add(current.left); }\n            if (current.right != null) { queue.add(current.right); }\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // Deletes the first node (in level order) holding value; returns false when it is absent.\n    public boolean delete(int value) {\n        if (root == null) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        Node target = null;\n        Node last = null;\n        Node lastParent = null;\n        Queue<Node> nodes = new LinkedList<>();\n        Queue<Node> parents = new LinkedList<>();\n        nodes.add(root);\n        parents.add(null);\n        while (!nodes.isEmpty()) {\n            Node current = nodes.remove();\n            Node parent = parents.remove();\n            if (target == null && current.value == value) {\n                target = current;\n            }\n            last = current;\n            lastParent = parent;\n            if (current.left != null) {\n                nodes.add(current.left);\n                parents.add(current);\n            }\n            if (current.right != null) {\n                nodes.add(current.right);\n                parents.add(current);\n            }\n        }\n        if (target == null) {\n            return false;\n        }\n        target.value = last.value;\n        if (last == root) {\n            root = null;\n        } else if (lastParent.right == last) {\n            lastParent.right = null;\n        } else {\n            lastParent.left = null;\n        }\n        size--;\n        return true;\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> inorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        inorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void inorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        inorder(node.left, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n        inorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> preorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        preorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void preorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        out.add(node.value);\n        preorder(node.left, out);\n        preorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> postorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        postorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void postorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        postorder(node.left, out);\n        postorder(node.right, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> levelOrder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) {\n            return result;\n        }\n        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        queue.add(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            Node node = queue.remove();\n            result.add(node.value);\n            if (node.left != null) { queue.add(node.left); }\n            if (node.right != null) { queue.add(node.right); }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BinaryTree tree = new BinaryTree();\n        for (int value : new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}) {\n            tree.insert(value);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Level order: \" + tree.levelOrder());\n        System.out.println(\"Inorder:     \" + tree.inorder());\n        System.out.println(\"Preorder:    \" + tree.preorder());\n        System.out.println(\"Postorder:   \" + tree.postorder());\n        tree.delete(2);\n        System.out.println(\"After deleting 2: \" + tree.levelOrder() + \", height \" + tree.height() + \", contains 6: \" + tree.contains(6));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"insert": [34, 38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 45, 46, 47, 50], "search": [60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 66, 67], "delete": [82, 83, 84, 85, 86, 87, 88, 90, 91, 104, 106, 108, 110], "inorder": [126, 127, 128], "preorder": [141, 142, 143], "postorder": [156, 157, 158], "levelOrder": [167, 168, 169, 170, 171, 172]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insert", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["delete", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"], ["inorder / preorder / postorder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(log n)"], ["levelOrder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "A general binary tree has no ordering rule. This implementation inserts each new value at the first free position in level order, so the tree stays complete, and deletes by copying the last node's value into the deleted node and removing the last node. Searching has to look at nodes level by level. The four traversals are the standard orders.", "why": "Without an ordering rule nothing can be skipped: finding the first free position, searching, and finding the last node for a delete all scan level by level, so they are O(n) (insert and search can finish early). Traversals visit each node once; the recursive ones use O(h) stack, and h is about log2 n because the tree stays complete. Level order keeps up to one level of nodes in a queue, O(n)."}, "graph_list": {"file": "AdjacencyListGraph.java", "code": "import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Arrays;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\n// Graph stored as adjacency lists. Vertices are numbered 0 .. vertexCount - 1; weights are non-negative integers.\npublic class AdjacencyListGraph {\n    public static final int INF = Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n\n    public static class Edge {\n        public final int from;\n        public final int to;\n        public final int weight;\n\n        Edge(int from, int to, int weight) {\n            this.from = from;\n            this.to = to;\n            this.weight = weight;\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public String toString() { return from + \"-\" + to + \" (\" + weight + \")\"; }\n    }\n\n    public static class ShortestPaths {\n        public final int[] distance; // INF when a vertex is unreachable\n        public final int[] previous; // -1 when a vertex has no predecessor\n\n        ShortestPaths(int[] distance, int[] previous) {\n            this.distance = distance;\n            this.previous = previous;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private int vertexCount;\n    private final boolean directed;\n    private final List<List<Edge>> adjacency = new ArrayList<>();\n    private final List<Edge> edges = new ArrayList<>(); // each edge once, in insertion order\n\n    public AdjacencyListGraph(int vertexCount, boolean directed) {\n        if (vertexCount < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"vertexCount must not be negative\");\n        }\n        this.directed = directed;\n        for (int i = 0; i < vertexCount; i++) {\n            adjacency.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        }\n        this.vertexCount = vertexCount;\n    }\n\n    public int addVertex() {\n        adjacency.add(new ArrayList<>());\n        return vertexCount++;\n    }\n\n    public void addEdge(int from, int to, int weight) {\n        checkVertex(from);\n        checkVertex(to);\n        if (weight < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Negative weights are not supported\");\n        }\n        Edge edge = new Edge(from, to, weight);\n        edges.add(edge);\n        adjacency.get(from).add(edge);\n        if (!directed) {\n            adjacency.get(to).add(new Edge(to, from, weight));\n        }\n    }\n\n    public int vertexCount() { return vertexCount; }\n\n    private void checkVertex(int v) {\n        if (v < 0 || v >= vertexCount) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"No such vertex: \" + v);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Breadth-first search: returns the vertices reachable from start, nearest first.\n    public List<Integer> bfs(int start) {\n        checkVertex(start);\n        List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[vertexCount];\n        Queue<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        visited[start] = true;\n        queue.add(start);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            int current = queue.remove();\n            order.add(current);\n            for (Edge edge : adjacency.get(current)) {\n                if (!visited[edge.to]) {\n                    visited[edge.to] = true;\n                    queue.add(edge.to);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return order;\n    }\n\n    // Depth-first search with an explicit stack. Neighbours are pushed in reverse so the first neighbour is explored first.\n    public List<Integer> dfs(int start) {\n        checkVertex(start);\n        List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();\n        boolean[] visited = new boolean[vertexCount];\n        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n        stack.push(start);\n        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {\n            int current = stack.pop();\n            if (visited[current]) {\n                continue;\n            }\n            visited[current] = true;\n            order.add(current);\n            List<Edge> neighbours = adjacency.get(current);\n            for (int i = neighbours.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n                if (!visited[neighbours.get(i).to]) {\n                    stack.push(neighbours.get(i).to);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return order;\n    }\n\n    // Dijkstra's algorithm, O(V^2) version (no priority queue). Weights must be non-negative.\n    public ShortestPaths dijkstra(int start) {\n        checkVertex(start);\n        int[] distance = new int[vertexCount];\n        int[] previous = new int[vertexCount];\n        boolean[] settled = new boolean[vertexCount];\n        Arrays.fill(distance, INF);\n        Arrays.fill(previous, -1);\n        distance[start] = 0;\n        for (int round = 0; round < vertexCount; round++) {\n            int u = -1;\n            for (int v = 0; v < vertexCount; v++) {\n                if (!settled[v] && (u == -1 || distance[v] < distance[u])) {\n                    u = v;\n                }\n            }\n            if (u == -1 || distance[u] == INF) {\n                break; // everything left is unreachable\n            }\n            settled[u] = true;\n            for (Edge edge : adjacency.get(u)) {\n                if ((long) distance[u] + edge.weight < distance[edge.to]) {\n                    distance[edge.to] = distance[u] + edge.weight;\n                    previous[edge.to] = u;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n        return new ShortestPaths(distance, previous);\n    }\n\n    // Prim's algorithm on an undirected graph: repeatedly adds the cheapest edge that leaves the tree.\n    public List<Edge> prim(int start) {\n        if (directed) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Prim's algorithm needs an undirected graph\");\n        }\n        checkVertex(start);\n        boolean[] inTree = new boolean[vertexCount];\n        List<Edge> tree = new ArrayList<>();\n        inTree[start] = true;\n        int covered = 1;\n        while (covered < vertexCount) {\n            Edge best = null;\n            for (Edge edge : edges) {\n                boolean crosses = inTree[edge.from] != inTree[edge.to];\n                if (crosses && (best == null || edge.weight < best.weight)) {\n                    best = edge;\n                }\n            }\n            if (best == null) {\n                break; // disconnected: only the start's component is covered\n            }\n            inTree[best.from] = true;\n            inTree[best.to] = true;\n            tree.add(best);\n            covered++;\n        }\n        return tree;\n    }\n\n    // Kruskal's algorithm on an undirected graph: cheapest edges first, skipping any edge that would close a cycle.\n    public List<Edge> kruskal() {\n        if (directed) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Kruskal's algorithm needs an undirected graph\");\n        }\n        List<Edge> sorted = new ArrayList<>(edges);\n        sorted.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e.weight));\n        int[] parent = new int[vertexCount];\n        for (int i = 0; i < vertexCount; i++) {\n            parent[i] = i;\n        }\n        List<Edge> tree = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Edge edge : sorted) {\n            int rootA = find(parent, edge.from);\n            int rootB = find(parent, edge.to);\n            if (rootA != rootB) {\n                parent[rootA] = rootB;\n                tree.add(edge);\n            }\n        }\n        return tree;\n    }\n\n    // Union-find root lookup with path halving.\n    private int find(int[] parent, int x) {\n        while (parent[x] != x) {\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];\n            x = parent[x];\n        }\n        return x;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        AdjacencyListGraph g = new AdjacencyListGraph(6, false); // vertices 0..5 stand for A..F\n        int[][] edgeList = {{0, 1, 4}, {0, 2, 2}, {1, 3, 5}, {2, 3, 8}, {2, 4, 10}, {3, 4, 2}, {3, 5, 6}, {4, 5, 3}};\n        for (int[] e : edgeList) {\n            g.addEdge(e[0], e[1], e[2]);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"BFS from A:  \" + g.bfs(0));\n        System.out.println(\"DFS from A:  \" + g.dfs(0));\n        ShortestPaths sp = g.dijkstra(0);\n        System.out.println(\"Distances:   \" + Arrays.toString(sp.distance));\n        System.out.println(\"Prim MST:    \" + g.prim(0));\n        System.out.println(\"Kruskal MST: \" + g.kruskal());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"addVertex": [55, 56], "addEdge": [66, 67, 69], "bfsStart": [87, 88], "bfsVisit": [89, 90, 91], "bfsExpand": [92, 93, 94, 95], "dfsStart": [108], "dfsVisit": [110, 111, 112, 114, 115], "dfsExpand": [117, 118, 119], "dijkInit": [132, 133, 134], "dijkSettle": [137, 138, 139, 142, 145], "dijkRelax": [146, 147, 148, 149], "primInit": [164], "primPick": [168, 169, 170, 171], "primAdd": [177, 178, 179], "kruskalSort": [191], "kruskalFind": [197, 198, 199, 200], "kruskalAccept": [201, 202]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {"bfsVisit": ["bfsVisit", "bfsExpand"], "dfsVisit": ["dfsVisit", "dfsExpand"], "dijkSettle": ["dijkSettle", "dijkRelax"], "primAdd": ["primPick", "primAdd"]}, "rows": [["addVertex", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["addEdge", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["bfs", "O(V)", "O(V + E)", "O(V + E)", "O(V)"], ["dfs", "O(V)", "O(V + E)", "O(V + E)", "O(V + E)"], ["dijkstra", "O(V)", "O(V²)", "O(V²)", "O(V)"], ["prim", "O(V)", "O(V·E)", "O(V·E)", "O(V)"], ["kruskal", "O(V + E log E)", "O(V + E log E)", "O(V + E log E)", "O(V + E)"]], "space": "O(V + E) for the adjacency lists and the edge list", "about": "Each vertex keeps a list of its outgoing edges, so visiting the neighbours of a vertex costs only its degree. The class also keeps one list of all edges for Prim and Kruskal. bfs uses a queue, dfs an explicit stack, dijkstra the simple O(V²) scan for the closest unsettled vertex, prim adds the cheapest edge leaving the tree each round, and kruskal sorts the edges and joins components with union-find.", "why": "bfs and dfs touch each vertex once and each adjacency entry once, so O(V + E); the visited array is O(V) even when nothing is reachable. This dijkstra scans all V vertices in each of up to V rounds, giving O(V²) (a priority queue would give O((V + E) log V)). prim rescans all E edges for each of the V − 1 vertices it adds. kruskal is dominated by sorting the E edges, and union-find with path halving is nearly constant per edge."}, "graph_matrix": {"file": "AdjacencyMatrixGraph.java", "code": "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\n// Graph stored as a V x V matrix: matrix[u][v] is the weight of edge u -> v, or NO_EDGE.\n// Edge lookup is O(1) but the matrix always uses O(V^2) space.\npublic class AdjacencyMatrixGraph {\n    public static final int NO_EDGE = -1;\n\n    private int[][] matrix;\n    private int vertexCount;\n    private final boolean directed;\n\n    public AdjacencyMatrixGraph(int vertexCount, boolean directed) {\n        if (vertexCount < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"vertexCount must not be negative\");\n        }\n        this.vertexCount = vertexCount;\n        this.directed = directed;\n        this.matrix = new int[vertexCount][vertexCount];\n        for (int[] row : matrix) {\n            java.util.Arrays.fill(row, NO_EDGE);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Grows the matrix by one row and one column.\n    public int addVertex() {\n        int[][] bigger = new int[vertexCount + 1][vertexCount + 1];\n        for (int i = 0; i <= vertexCount; i++) {\n            for (int j = 0; j <= vertexCount; j++) {\n                bigger[i][j] = (i < vertexCount && j < vertexCount) ? matrix[i][j] : NO_EDGE;\n            }\n        }\n        matrix = bigger;\n        return vertexCount++;\n    }\n\n    public void addEdge(int from, int to, int weight) {\n        checkVertex(from);\n        checkVertex(to);\n        if (weight < 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Negative weights are not supported\");\n        }\n        matrix[from][to] = weight;\n        if (!directed) {\n            matrix[to][from] = weight;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void removeEdge(int from, int to) {\n        checkVertex(from);\n        checkVertex(to);\n        matrix[from][to] = NO_EDGE;\n        if (!directed) {\n            matrix[to][from] = NO_EDGE;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public boolean hasEdge(int from, int to) {\n        checkVertex(from);\n        checkVertex(to);\n        return matrix[from][to] != NO_EDGE;\n    }\n\n    public int weight(int from, int to) {\n        checkVertex(from);\n        checkVertex(to);\n        return matrix[from][to];\n    }\n\n    // Vertices reachable from v by one edge, in increasing order.\n    public List<Integer> neighbours(int v) {\n        checkVertex(v);\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int u = 0; u < vertexCount; u++) {\n            if (matrix[v][u] != NO_EDGE) {\n                result.add(u);\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public int vertexCount() { return vertexCount; }\n\n    private void checkVertex(int v) {\n        if (v < 0 || v >= vertexCount) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"No such vertex: \" + v);\n        }\n    }\n\n    public void print() {\n        for (int[] row : matrix) {\n            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();\n            for (int cell : row) {\n                line.append(cell == NO_EDGE ? \" .\" : String.format(\"%2d\", cell)).append(' ');\n            }\n            System.out.println(line.toString().stripTrailing());\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        AdjacencyMatrixGraph g = new AdjacencyMatrixGraph(4, false);\n        g.addEdge(0, 1, 5);\n        g.addEdge(1, 2, 3);\n        g.addEdge(2, 3, 7);\n        g.addVertex(); // vertex 4\n        g.addEdge(3, 4, 1);\n        g.print();\n        System.out.println(\"Neighbours of 3: \" + g.neighbours(3) + \", edge 0-2? \" + g.hasEdge(0, 2));\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"addVertex": [27, 28, 29, 30, 33, 34], "addEdge": [43, 45]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["addVertex", "O(V²)", "O(V²)", "O(V²)", "O(V²)"], ["addEdge", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["removeEdge", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["hasEdge / weight", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["neighbours", "O(V)", "O(V)", "O(V)", "O(V)"], ["print", "O(V²)", "O(V²)", "O(V²)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(V²) for the matrix", "about": "The graph is a V x V matrix where matrix[u][v] holds the weight of the edge u -> v, or -1 when there is no edge. Checking or changing a single edge is one array access, but the matrix uses O(V²) space even for a sparse graph, and finding all neighbours of a vertex means scanning a full row. The graph algorithms in this app are shown on the adjacency list version.", "why": "Edge operations index the matrix directly, so they are O(1). neighbours scans one row of V cells. addVertex must allocate a new (V + 1) x (V + 1) matrix and copy every cell, which costs O(V²). The matrix itself is O(V²) regardless of how many edges exist."}, "tree_bst": {"file": "BinarySearchTree.java", "code": "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class BinarySearchTree {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        Node left;\n        Node right;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node root;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return root == null; }\n\n    public int height() { return height(root); }\n\n    private int height(Node node) {\n        return node == null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));\n    }\n\n    // Inserts value; duplicates are ignored.\n    public void insert(int value) {\n        root = insert(root, value);\n    }\n\n    private Node insert(Node node, int value) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return new Node(value);\n        }\n        if (value < node.value) {\n            node.left = insert(node.left, value);\n        } else if (value > node.value) {\n            node.right = insert(node.right, value);\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    public boolean contains(int value) {\n        Node current = root;\n        while (current != null) {\n            if (value == current.value) {\n                return true;\n            }\n            current = value < current.value ? current.left : current.right;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // Deletes value if present. A node with two children is replaced by its inorder successor.\n    public void delete(int value) {\n        root = delete(root, value);\n    }\n\n    private Node delete(Node node, int value) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return null; // value not found\n        }\n        if (value < node.value) {\n            node.left = delete(node.left, value);\n        } else if (value > node.value) {\n            node.right = delete(node.right, value);\n        } else {\n            if (node.left == null) {\n                return node.right;\n            }\n            if (node.right == null) {\n                return node.left;\n            }\n            Node successor = node.right;\n            while (successor.left != null) {\n                successor = successor.left;\n            }\n            node.value = successor.value;\n            node.right = delete(node.right, successor.value);\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> inorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        inorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void inorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        inorder(node.left, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n        inorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> preorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        preorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void preorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        out.add(node.value);\n        preorder(node.left, out);\n        preorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> postorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        postorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void postorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        postorder(node.left, out);\n        postorder(node.right, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> levelOrder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) {\n            return result;\n        }\n        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        queue.add(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            Node node = queue.remove();\n            result.add(node.value);\n            if (node.left != null) { queue.add(node.left); }\n            if (node.right != null) { queue.add(node.right); }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        BinarySearchTree tree = new BinarySearchTree();\n        for (int value : new int[]{50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80}) {\n            tree.insert(value);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Inorder:   \" + tree.inorder());\n        System.out.println(\"Preorder:  \" + tree.preorder());\n        System.out.println(\"Postorder: \" + tree.postorder());\n        System.out.println(\"Level:     \" + tree.levelOrder());\n        tree.delete(50);\n        System.out.println(\"After deleting 50: \" + tree.inorder() + \", contains 60: \" + tree.contains(60) + \", height \" + tree.height());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"insert": [32, 34, 35, 36, 37], "search": [44, 45, 46, 48], "delete": [62, 63, 64, 65, 67, 68, 70, 71, 73, 74, 75, 77, 78], "inorder": [93, 94, 95], "preorder": [108, 109, 110], "postorder": [123, 124, 125], "levelOrder": [134, 135, 136, 137, 138, 139]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insert", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(h)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["delete", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(n)", "O(h)"], ["inorder / preorder / postorder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(h)"], ["levelOrder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(w)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "A binary search tree keeps smaller values in the left subtree and larger values in the right subtree of every node, so inorder traversal visits values in ascending order. Duplicates are ignored. Deleting a node with two children replaces it with its inorder successor (the smallest value in the right subtree).", "why": "Insert, search and delete follow one root-to-leaf path, so they cost the tree height h: about log n when the tree is balanced, but n when values arrive in sorted order and the tree becomes a chain. The recursive methods use O(h) stack space; contains is iterative. Traversals visit every node once, and levelOrder holds up to one level of nodes (width w) in its queue."}, "tree_avl": {"file": "AVLTree.java", "code": "import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.LinkedList;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\n// Self-balancing BST: after every insert or delete, subtree heights differ by at most 1.\npublic class AVLTree {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        int height = 1;\n        Node left;\n        Node right;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node root;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return root == null; }\n\n    public int height() { return height(root); }\n\n    private int height(Node node) {\n        return node == null ? 0 : node.height;\n    }\n\n    private void updateHeight(Node node) {\n        node.height = 1 + Math.max(height(node.left), height(node.right));\n    }\n\n    // Positive: left-heavy, negative: right-heavy. Balanced trees stay in [-1, 1].\n    private int balanceFactor(Node node) {\n        return node == null ? 0 : height(node.left) - height(node.right);\n    }\n\n    // Right rotation around y: its left child x becomes the subtree root.\n    private Node rotateRight(Node y) {\n        Node x = y.left;\n        Node moved = x.right;\n        x.right = y;\n        y.left = moved;\n        updateHeight(y);\n        updateHeight(x);\n        return x;\n    }\n\n    // Left rotation around x: its right child y becomes the subtree root.\n    private Node rotateLeft(Node x) {\n        Node y = x.right;\n        Node moved = y.left;\n        y.left = x;\n        x.right = moved;\n        updateHeight(x);\n        updateHeight(y);\n        return y;\n    }\n\n    // Restores the AVL property at node: LL, LR, RR or RL case.\n    private Node rebalance(Node node) {\n        updateHeight(node);\n        int balance = balanceFactor(node);\n        if (balance > 1 && balanceFactor(node.left) >= 0) {\n            return rotateRight(node);\n        }\n        if (balance > 1) {\n            node.left = rotateLeft(node.left);\n            return rotateRight(node);\n        }\n        if (balance < -1 && balanceFactor(node.right) <= 0) {\n            return rotateLeft(node);\n        }\n        if (balance < -1) {\n            node.right = rotateRight(node.right);\n            return rotateLeft(node);\n        }\n        return node;\n    }\n\n    // Inserts value; duplicates are ignored.\n    public void insert(int value) {\n        root = insert(root, value);\n    }\n\n    private Node insert(Node node, int value) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return new Node(value);\n        }\n        if (value < node.value) {\n            node.left = insert(node.left, value);\n        } else if (value > node.value) {\n            node.right = insert(node.right, value);\n        } else {\n            return node; // duplicate\n        }\n        return rebalance(node);\n    }\n\n    public boolean contains(int value) {\n        Node current = root;\n        while (current != null) {\n            if (value == current.value) {\n                return true;\n            }\n            current = value < current.value ? current.left : current.right;\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    // Deletes value if present; a node with two children is replaced by its inorder successor.\n    public void delete(int value) {\n        root = delete(root, value);\n    }\n\n    private Node delete(Node node, int value) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return null; // value not found\n        }\n        if (value < node.value) {\n            node.left = delete(node.left, value);\n        } else if (value > node.value) {\n            node.right = delete(node.right, value);\n        } else {\n            if (node.left == null) {\n                return node.right;\n            }\n            if (node.right == null) {\n                return node.left;\n            }\n            Node successor = node.right;\n            while (successor.left != null) {\n                successor = successor.left;\n            }\n            node.value = successor.value;\n            node.right = delete(node.right, successor.value);\n        }\n        return rebalance(node);\n    }\n\n    // True when every node satisfies |balance factor| <= 1 (used to check the implementation).\n    public boolean isBalanced() { return isBalanced(root); }\n\n    private boolean isBalanced(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return true;\n        }\n        int actual = 1 + Math.max(heightOf(node.left), heightOf(node.right));\n        return actual == node.height && Math.abs(balanceFactor(node)) <= 1 && isBalanced(node.left) && isBalanced(node.right);\n    }\n\n    private int heightOf(Node node) {\n        return node == null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(heightOf(node.left), heightOf(node.right));\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> inorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        inorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void inorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        inorder(node.left, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n        inorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> preorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        preorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void preorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        out.add(node.value);\n        preorder(node.left, out);\n        preorder(node.right, out);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> postorder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        postorder(root, result);\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    private void postorder(Node node, List<Integer> out) {\n        if (node == null) {\n            return;\n        }\n        postorder(node.left, out);\n        postorder(node.right, out);\n        out.add(node.value);\n    }\n\n    public List<Integer> levelOrder() {\n        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (root == null) {\n            return result;\n        }\n        Queue<Node> queue = new LinkedList<>();\n        queue.add(root);\n        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n            Node node = queue.remove();\n            result.add(node.value);\n            if (node.left != null) { queue.add(node.left); }\n            if (node.right != null) { queue.add(node.right); }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        AVLTree tree = new AVLTree();\n        for (int value : new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 25}) { // 10, 20, 30 triggers an RR rotation\n            tree.insert(value);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Preorder: \" + tree.preorder() + \", height \" + tree.height() + \", balanced \" + tree.isBalanced());\n        tree.delete(40);\n        System.out.println(\"After deleting 40: \" + tree.inorder() + \", height \" + tree.height());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"rotR": [38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43, 44], "rotL": [49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55], "rebalance": [60, 61], "rotLL": [62, 63], "rotLR": [65, 66, 67], "rotRR": [69, 70], "rotRL": [72, 73, 74], "insert": [86, 88, 89, 90, 91, 95], "search": [100, 101, 102, 104], "delete": [118, 119, 120, 121, 123, 124, 126, 127, 129, 130, 131, 133, 134, 136], "inorder": [164, 165, 166], "preorder": [179, 180, 181], "postorder": [194, 195, 196], "levelOrder": [205, 206, 207, 208, 209, 210]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {"rotLL": ["rotLL", "rotR"], "rotRR": ["rotRR", "rotL"], "rotLR": ["rotLR", "rotL", "rotR"], "rotRL": ["rotRL", "rotR", "rotL"]}, "rows": [["insert", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)", "O(1)"], ["delete", "O(1)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)", "O(log n)"], ["inorder / preorder / postorder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(log n)"], ["levelOrder", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(w)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "An AVL tree is a binary search tree that rebalances after every insert and delete so the heights of the two subtrees of any node differ by at most 1. rebalance computes the balance factor and applies one of four rotations: LL (right rotation), RR (left rotation), LR (left then right) or RL (right then left).", "why": "Rebalancing keeps the height at about 1.44 log2 n, so insert, search and delete are O(log n) even in the worst case. A rotation relinks a constant number of nodes in O(1). The recursive methods use O(log n) stack space; traversals visit all n nodes."}, "stack_array": {"file": "ArrayStack.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class ArrayStack {\n    private final int[] data;\n    private int top = -1; // index of the top element, -1 when the stack is empty\n\n    public ArrayStack(int capacity) {\n        if (capacity <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Capacity must be positive\");\n        }\n        data = new int[capacity];\n    }\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return top == -1; }\n\n    public boolean isFull() { return top == data.length - 1; }\n\n    public int size() { return top + 1; }\n\n    public void push(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Stack overflow\");\n        }\n        data[++top] = value;\n    }\n\n    public int pop() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Stack underflow\");\n        }\n        return data[top--];\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Stack is empty\");\n        }\n        return data[top];\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { return Arrays.copyOf(data, size()); } // bottom to top\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ArrayStack stack = new ArrayStack(3);\n        stack.push(10);\n        stack.push(20);\n        stack.push(30);\n        System.out.println(\"Stack: \" + Arrays.toString(stack.toArray()) + \", top = \" + stack.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"Popped \" + stack.pop() + \", size = \" + stack.size());\n        try {\n            stack.push(1);\n            stack.push(2); // exceeds the capacity of 3\n        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"Error: \" + e.getMessage());\n        }\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"overflow": [22], "push": [24], "underflow": [29], "pop": [31], "peek": [38]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["push", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["pop", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the array", "about": "A fixed-capacity stack stored in an array. top is the index of the newest element (-1 when empty). push increments top and pop decrements it, so the last value pushed is the first one popped. Overflow and underflow throw IllegalStateException.", "why": "Every operation reads or writes only data[top] and updates one counter, so the cost does not depend on how many values are stored. The only storage is the array of capacity n."}, "stack_linked": {"file": "LinkedStack.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class LinkedStack {\n    private static class Node {\n        final int value;\n        final Node next;\n\n        Node(int value, Node next) {\n            this.value = value;\n            this.next = next;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private Node head; // the top of the stack\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return head == null; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void push(int value) {\n        head = new Node(value, head);\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int pop() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Stack underflow\");\n        }\n        int value = head.value;\n        head = head.next;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Stack is empty\");\n        }\n        return head.value;\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { // bottom to top\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        int i = size - 1;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            result[i--] = current.value;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LinkedStack stack = new LinkedStack();\n        stack.push(10);\n        stack.push(20);\n        stack.push(30);\n        System.out.println(\"Stack: \" + Arrays.toString(stack.toArray()) + \", top = \" + stack.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"Popped \" + stack.pop() + \", size = \" + stack.size());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"push": [22, 23], "underflow": [28], "pop": [31, 32], "peek": [40]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["push", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["pop", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "A stack built from immutable nodes. head is the top: push creates a node that points at the old head, and pop moves head to head.next. There is no capacity limit.", "why": "Each operation changes only the head reference and the size counter. Each stored value costs one node, so total space grows with the number of values, but each operation needs O(1) extra space."}, "queue_array": {"file": "ArrayQueue.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\n// Simple array queue: the front is always index 0, so dequeue shifts the remaining values left (O(n)).\npublic class ArrayQueue {\n    private final int[] data;\n    private int size;\n\n    public ArrayQueue(int capacity) {\n        if (capacity <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Capacity must be positive\");\n        }\n        data = new int[capacity];\n    }\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public boolean isFull() { return size == data.length; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void enqueue(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue overflow\");\n        }\n        data[size++] = value;\n    }\n\n    public int dequeue() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue underflow\");\n        }\n        int value = data[0];\n        for (int i = 1; i < size; i++) {\n            data[i - 1] = data[i];\n        }\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue is empty\");\n        }\n        return data[0];\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { return Arrays.copyOf(data, size); } // front to rear\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ArrayQueue queue = new ArrayQueue(4);\n        queue.enqueue(10);\n        queue.enqueue(20);\n        queue.enqueue(30);\n        System.out.println(\"Queue: \" + Arrays.toString(queue.toArray()) + \", front = \" + queue.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"Dequeued \" + queue.dequeue() + \", size = \" + queue.size());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"overflow": [23], "enqueue": [25], "underflow": [30], "dequeue": [33, 34, 36], "peek": [44]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["enqueue", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["dequeue", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the array", "about": "A queue in a fixed array whose front is always index 0. enqueue writes at index size; dequeue returns data[0] and shifts every remaining value one place left. Simple, but dequeue is slow for large queues; see CircularQueue.", "why": "enqueue writes one slot. dequeue copies the remaining size − 1 values, so it costs O(n) in general and O(1) only when the queue holds a single value."}, "queue_circular": {"file": "CircularQueue.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\n// Circular array queue: indices wrap around with %, so enqueue and dequeue are both O(1).\npublic class CircularQueue {\n    private final int[] data;\n    private int front; // index of the first element\n    private int size;\n\n    public CircularQueue(int capacity) {\n        if (capacity <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Capacity must be positive\");\n        }\n        data = new int[capacity];\n    }\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public boolean isFull() { return size == data.length; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void enqueue(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue overflow\");\n        }\n        int rear = (front + size) % data.length; // first free slot, wrapping around\n        data[rear] = value;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int dequeue() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue underflow\");\n        }\n        int value = data[front];\n        front = (front + 1) % data.length;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (isEmpty()) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue is empty\");\n        }\n        return data[front];\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { // front to rear\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            result[i] = data[(front + i) % data.length];\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CircularQueue queue = new CircularQueue(3);\n        queue.enqueue(10);\n        queue.enqueue(20);\n        queue.enqueue(30);\n        queue.dequeue();\n        queue.enqueue(40); // reuses the slot freed at index 0\n        System.out.println(\"Queue: \" + Arrays.toString(queue.toArray()) + \", front = \" + queue.peek());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"overflow": [24], "enqueue": [27, 28], "underflow": [33], "dequeue": [36, 37], "peek": [45]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["enqueue", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["dequeue", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the array", "about": "A queue in a fixed array that wraps around. front is the index of the first value and the next free slot is (front + size) % capacity, so slots freed by dequeue are reused and nothing is shifted.", "why": "Both ends move by changing one index with the % operator and the size counter, so no values are copied. Storage is the fixed array of capacity n."}, "queue_linked": {"file": "LinkedQueue.java", "code": "import java.util.Arrays;\n\npublic class LinkedQueue {\n    private static class Node {\n        final int value;\n        Node next;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node head; // front of the queue\n    private Node tail; // rear of the queue\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return head == null; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void enqueue(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        if (tail == null) {\n            head = node;\n        } else {\n            tail.next = node;\n        }\n        tail = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int dequeue() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue underflow\");\n        }\n        int value = head.value;\n        head = head.next;\n        if (head == null) {\n            tail = null; // the queue became empty\n        }\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int peek() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"Queue is empty\");\n        }\n        return head.value;\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() { // front to rear\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        int i = 0;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            result[i++] = current.value;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LinkedQueue queue = new LinkedQueue();\n        queue.enqueue(10);\n        queue.enqueue(20);\n        queue.enqueue(30);\n        System.out.println(\"Queue: \" + Arrays.toString(queue.toArray()) + \", front = \" + queue.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"Dequeued \" + queue.dequeue() + \", size = \" + queue.size());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"enqueue": [22, 24, 26, 27], "underflow": [32], "dequeue": [35, 37, 39], "peek": [47]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["enqueue", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["dequeue", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["peek", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "A queue made of linked nodes with references to both head (front) and tail (rear). enqueue appends after tail and dequeue removes the head; when the last node is removed, tail is reset to null.", "why": "Keeping a tail reference means enqueue never walks the list, and dequeue only moves head. Each stored value costs one node."}, "list_singly": {"file": "SinglyLinkedList.java", "code": "import java.util.NoSuchElementException;\n\npublic class SinglyLinkedList {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        Node next;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node head;\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void insertHead(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        node.next = head;\n        head = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public void insertTail(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        if (head == null) {\n            head = node;\n        } else {\n            Node current = head;\n            while (current.next != null) {\n                current = current.next;\n            }\n            current.next = node;\n        }\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    // Inserts so that the new node ends up at position index (0 = head, size = tail).\n    public void insertAt(int index, int value) {\n        if (index < 0 || index > size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            insertHead(value);\n            return;\n        }\n        Node previous = head;\n        for (int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n            previous = previous.next;\n        }\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        node.next = previous.next;\n        previous.next = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteHead() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        int value = head.value;\n        head = head.next;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteTail() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        if (head.next == null) {\n            return deleteHead(); // only one node\n        }\n        Node previous = head;\n        while (previous.next.next != null) {\n            previous = previous.next;\n        }\n        int value = previous.next.value;\n        previous.next = null;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteAt(int index) {\n        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            return deleteHead();\n        }\n        Node previous = head;\n        for (int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n            previous = previous.next;\n        }\n        int value = previous.next.value;\n        previous.next = previous.next.next;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    // Returns the index of the first node holding value, or -1.\n    public int search(int value) {\n        int index = 0;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            if (current.value == value) {\n                return index;\n            }\n            index++;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public void reverse() {\n        Node previous = null;\n        Node current = head;\n        while (current != null) {\n            Node next = current.next;\n            current.next = previous;\n            previous = current;\n            current = next;\n        }\n        head = previous;\n    }\n\n    public void traverse() {\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            System.out.print(current.value + \" -> \");\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"null\");\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() {\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        int i = 0;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            result[i++] = current.value;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        SinglyLinkedList list = new SinglyLinkedList();\n        list.insertTail(20);\n        list.insertTail(30);\n        list.insertHead(10);\n        list.insertAt(2, 25);\n        list.traverse();                       // 10 -> 20 -> 25 -> 30 -> null\n        list.reverse();\n        list.traverse();                       // 30 -> 25 -> 20 -> 10 -> null\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 20: \" + list.search(20) + \", deleted tail: \" + list.deleteTail());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"insertHead": [20, 21, 22], "insertTail": [28, 31, 32, 34, 36], "insertAt": [49, 50, 53, 54, 55], "underflow": [60, 70], "deleteHead": [63, 64], "deleteTail": [76, 77, 80, 81], "deleteAt": [93, 94, 97, 98], "search": [105, 106, 107], "reverse": [117, 118, 119, 120, 121, 123], "traverse": [127, 128]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insertHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertTail", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["insertAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["deleteTail", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["reverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["traverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "Each node stores a value and a next reference; only head is kept. Operations at the head are O(1), but reaching the tail or an index means walking from the head. reverse relinks every node in one pass.", "why": "insertHead and deleteHead touch only head. Anything at the tail or at an index must follow next references, which costs up to n steps. search, reverse and traverse visit every node once, and they need only a few extra references."}, "list_doubly": {"file": "DoublyLinkedList.java", "code": "import java.util.NoSuchElementException;\n\npublic class DoublyLinkedList {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        Node prev;\n        Node next;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node head;\n    private Node tail;\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void insertHead(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        node.next = head;\n        if (head != null) {\n            head.prev = node;\n        } else {\n            tail = node;\n        }\n        head = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public void insertTail(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        node.prev = tail;\n        if (tail != null) {\n            tail.next = node;\n        } else {\n            head = node;\n        }\n        tail = node;\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    // Inserts so that the new node ends up at position index (0 = head, size = tail).\n    public void insertAt(int index, int value) {\n        if (index < 0 || index > size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            insertHead(value);\n        } else if (index == size) {\n            insertTail(value);\n        } else {\n            Node current = head;\n            for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {\n                current = current.next;\n            }\n            Node node = new Node(value);\n            node.prev = current.prev;\n            node.next = current;\n            current.prev.next = node;\n            current.prev = node;\n            size++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public int deleteHead() {\n        if (head == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        int value = head.value;\n        head = head.next;\n        if (head != null) {\n            head.prev = null;\n        } else {\n            tail = null;\n        }\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteTail() {\n        if (tail == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        int value = tail.value;\n        tail = tail.prev;\n        if (tail != null) {\n            tail.next = null;\n        } else {\n            head = null;\n        }\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteAt(int index) {\n        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            return deleteHead();\n        }\n        if (index == size - 1) {\n            return deleteTail();\n        }\n        Node current = head;\n        for (int i = 0; i < index; i++) {\n            current = current.next;\n        }\n        current.prev.next = current.next;\n        current.next.prev = current.prev;\n        size--;\n        return current.value;\n    }\n\n    public int search(int value) {\n        int index = 0;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            if (current.value == value) {\n                return index;\n            }\n            index++;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    // Swaps prev and next in every node, then swaps head and tail.\n    public void reverse() {\n        Node current = head;\n        while (current != null) {\n            Node next = current.next;\n            current.next = current.prev;\n            current.prev = next;\n            current = next;\n        }\n        Node temp = head;\n        head = tail;\n        tail = temp;\n    }\n\n    public void traverse() {\n        System.out.print(\"null <-> \");\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            System.out.print(current.value + \" <-> \");\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"null\");\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() {\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        int i = 0;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next) {\n            result[i++] = current.value;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DoublyLinkedList list = new DoublyLinkedList();\n        list.insertTail(20);\n        list.insertTail(30);\n        list.insertHead(10);\n        list.insertAt(2, 25);\n        list.traverse();\n        list.reverse();\n        list.traverse();\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 20: \" + list.search(20) + \", deleted tail: \" + list.deleteTail());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"insertHead": [22, 24, 26, 28, 29], "insertTail": [34, 36, 38, 40, 41], "insertAt": [55, 56, 59, 60, 61, 62, 63], "underflow": [69, 84], "deleteHead": [72, 74, 76, 78], "deleteTail": [87, 89, 91, 93], "deleteAt": [108, 109, 111, 112, 113], "search": [119, 120, 121], "reverse": [131, 132, 133, 134, 135, 137, 138, 139], "traverse": [144, 145]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insertHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertTail", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["deleteTail", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["reverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["traverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "Each node has prev and next references and the list keeps both head and tail, so both ends can be inserted into and deleted from in O(1). reverse swaps prev and next in every node, then swaps head and tail.", "why": "Head and tail are directly reachable and prev lets deleteTail step back without a walk, so end operations are O(1). Indexed operations still walk up to n nodes. Every node stores two references."}, "list_circular": {"file": "CircularLinkedList.java", "code": "import java.util.NoSuchElementException;\n\n// Circular singly linked list that stores only tail; the head is tail.next, so both ends are O(1) to reach.\npublic class CircularLinkedList {\n    private static class Node {\n        int value;\n        Node next;\n\n        Node(int value) { this.value = value; }\n    }\n\n    private Node tail;\n    private int size;\n\n    public boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n\n    public int size() { return size; }\n\n    public void insertHead(int value) {\n        Node node = new Node(value);\n        if (tail == null) {\n            node.next = node;\n            tail = node;\n        } else {\n            node.next = tail.next;\n            tail.next = node;\n        }\n        size++;\n    }\n\n    public void insertTail(int value) {\n        insertHead(value);\n        tail = tail.next;\n    }\n\n    // Inserts so that the new node ends up at position index (0 = head, size = tail).\n    public void insertAt(int index, int value) {\n        if (index < 0 || index > size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            insertHead(value);\n        } else if (index == size) {\n            insertTail(value);\n        } else {\n            Node previous = tail.next;\n            for (int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n                previous = previous.next;\n            }\n            Node node = new Node(value);\n            node.next = previous.next;\n            previous.next = node;\n            size++;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public int deleteHead() {\n        if (tail == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        Node head = tail.next;\n        if (head == tail) {\n            tail = null;\n        } else {\n            tail.next = head.next;\n        }\n        size--;\n        return head.value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteTail() {\n        if (tail == null) {\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\"List is empty\");\n        }\n        if (size == 1) {\n            return deleteHead();\n        }\n        Node previous = tail.next;\n        while (previous.next != tail) {\n            previous = previous.next;\n        }\n        int value = tail.value;\n        previous.next = tail.next;\n        tail = previous;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int deleteAt(int index) {\n        if (index < 0 || index >= size) {\n            throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(\"Index: \" + index + \", size: \" + size);\n        }\n        if (index == 0) {\n            return deleteHead();\n        }\n        if (index == size - 1) {\n            return deleteTail();\n        }\n        Node previous = tail.next;\n        for (int i = 0; i < index - 1; i++) {\n            previous = previous.next;\n        }\n        int value = previous.next.value;\n        previous.next = previous.next.next;\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public int search(int value) {\n        if (tail == null) {\n            return -1;\n        }\n        Node current = tail.next;\n        for (int index = 0; index < size; index++) {\n            if (current.value == value) {\n                return index;\n            }\n            current = current.next;\n        }\n        return -1;\n    }\n\n    public void reverse() {\n        if (size < 2) {\n            return;\n        }\n        Node head = tail.next;\n        Node previous = tail;\n        Node current = head;\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            Node next = current.next;\n            current.next = previous;\n            previous = current;\n            current = next;\n        }\n        tail = head; // the old head is now the tail\n    }\n\n    public void traverse() {\n        if (tail != null) {\n            Node current = tail.next;\n            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n                System.out.print(current.value + \" -> \");\n                current = current.next;\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"(back to head)\");\n    }\n\n    public int[] toArray() {\n        int[] result = new int[size];\n        Node current = tail == null ? null : tail.next;\n        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n            result[i] = current.value;\n            current = current.next;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CircularLinkedList list = new CircularLinkedList();\n        list.insertTail(20);\n        list.insertTail(30);\n        list.insertHead(10);\n        list.insertAt(2, 25);\n        list.traverse();\n        list.reverse();\n        list.traverse();\n        System.out.println(\"Index of 20: \" + list.search(20) + \", deleted tail: \" + list.deleteTail());\n    }\n}\n", "marks": {"insertHead": [22, 23, 25, 26, 28], "insertTail": [33], "insertAt": [47, 48, 51, 52, 53], "underflow": [59, 73], "deleteHead": [63, 65, 67], "deleteTail": [79, 80, 83, 84, 85], "deleteAt": [100, 101, 104, 105], "search": [114, 115, 116], "reverse": [130, 131, 132, 133, 134, 136], "traverse": [142, 143]}, "kind": "ops", "lineEv": {}, "evMarks": {}, "rows": [["insertHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertTail", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["insertAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteHead", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)", "O(1)"], ["deleteTail", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["deleteAt", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["search", "O(1)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["reverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"], ["traverse", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(n)", "O(1)"]], "space": "O(n) for the nodes", "about": "A singly linked circular list that stores only tail; tail.next is the head, so the last node points back to the first. Both insertHead and insertTail are O(1). Traversals must stop after size nodes because there is no null end.", "why": "tail gives O(1) access to both ends, so insertHead, insertTail and deleteHead are constant time. deleteTail must find the node before tail, which takes up to n steps, as do indexed operations."}};
